      "source": [
        "**Your answer:**\n",
        "⚠ *Use your own curves and threshold results.*\n",
        "\n",
        "**3.1:** AUC measures how well the model **ranks** patients (does a stroke patient usually get a higher score than a healthy one?) across **all** possible thresholds, so a model can rank well (AUC [__] ≫ 0.5) and still have a very low recall at the default threshold 0.5. With rare strokes, even high-risk patients get probabilities well below 0.5 (e.g. 0.1–0.3), so the 0.5 cut-off almost never predicts \"stroke\". The ranking is good; the threshold is the problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "**3.2:** The Precision-Recall curve is more informative here. The ROC curve looks good because the huge number of healthy patients keeps the false positive rate small even when there are many false alarms. The PR curve uses precision, which is directly hurt by false alarms, and its no-skill line sits at the stroke rate ([__]), so we can see clearly how far above \"no skill\" the models are ([__] average precision for the best model).\n",
        "\n",
        "**3.3:** The chosen threshold ([__]) gives a test recall of [__], catching [__] of [__] strokes, but precision falls to [__] and [__] patients are flagged, of whom [__] are false alarms. *In favour:* missing a stroke is far worse than an extra check-up, and many false alarms can be handled with cheap follow-up tests. *Against:* flagging [__]% of all patients is costly, may overload clinicians and cause unnecessary worry, and most flagged patients ([__]%) do not have a stroke."
      ],
